spec:
  grid: square, N*(S+1)-1 per side; N=4-7 cells, S=3-5 cell size, total 19-29
  colors:
    bg: 0 (black)
    lattice: single color from {1,2,5,6,7,8,9} (avoids 3 green / 4 yellow)
  lattice:
    1-pixel-thick horizontal and vertical lines at positions S, 2S+1, 3S+2, ...
    margins (top/bottom/left/right) all equal S -- no outer border frame
    cell interiors are pure bg (no fg pixels inside cells)
  gaps:
    each gap is 1-2 bg pixels replacing fg on a lattice-line segment between two adjacent cell interiors
    gaps do NOT occur at lattice-line intersections [observed, 1 exception in test_0]
    25-33% of cell-edge segments are breached
    breaches must connect some cells into non-rectangular merged bg regions
  output:
    lattice pixels preserved; no bg remains
    each 4-connected bg region recolored green(3) if area == bbox area, else yellow(4)
    must have >=1 green and >=1 yellow region

generation_strategy:
  approach: build lattice first, then punch gaps to ensure both intact and merged cells
  steps:
    1. pick N in 4-7, S in 3-5; grid_size = N*(S+1)-1
    2. fill grid with bg; draw full 1px lattice lines at row/col positions S+k*(S+1) for k=0..N-2
    3. enumerate all cell-edge segments (2*N*(N-1) total); pick ~25-33% to breach
    4. for each breached edge, remove 1-2 fg pixels (within the cell-interior span, not at intersections)
    5. compute 4-connected bg components; verify at least 1 rectangular and 1 non-rectangular exist
       if not, adjust breaches (add/remove) and retry (low rejection -- random breaches almost always yield both types)
    6. pick lattice color randomly from {1,2,5,6,7,8,9}
```
